From 42960308abe1fd986a478de4f39a2cf4c477adc0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Zenium Port <port@zenium.invalid>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 07:01:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 91/91] soc: mediatek: answer the three record-mode symbols the display path layer calls

0090 landed ddp_path.c and, with it, three open names: cmdqRecWrite,
cmdqRecWaitNoClear and cmdqRecSetEventToken. They are the record-mode half of the
display path - writes and event ops appended to a recorded request instead of issued
by the CPU - and they are the reason the record layer was measured before it was
written, not after.

drivers/soc/mediatek/mtk-cmdq-disp-record.c defines those three, and nothing else.
Each one is the vendor engine's own function, narrowed to what a landed display object
can ask for:

  cmdqRecWrite()        = cmdq_op_write_reg() (v3/cmdq_record.c:1368) plus the subsys
                          resolution and field packing of cmdq_append_rw_s_command()
                          (:847), delegated to cmdq_pkt_write_s_value() /
                          cmdq_pkt_write_s_mask_value()
  cmdqRecWaitNoClear()  = cmdq_op_wait_no_clear() (:1510) -> cmdq_pkt_wait_no_clear()
  cmdqRecSetEventToken()= cmdq_op_set_event() (:1532) -> cmdq_pkt_set_event()

The two event entry points are delegations in the vendor too. The write entry point is
where the vendor and mainline's helper could have diverged, so the divergence is measured
rather than argued: upstream-port/tests/mtk_disp_record_host_check.c builds both sides'
instruction words for the addresses this tree can actually produce and every mask the
DISP_REG_SET() macro can pass, and reports 55 cases / 0 mismatches. It also parses the
instruction numbers out of both trees' include/linux/mailbox/mtk-cmdq-mailbox.h and
prints them, which is what makes "MASK here and MOVE there are one instruction" a
measurement: 0x02 on both sides, and 0x91 for WRITE_S_MASK against the vendor's
WRITE_S_W_MASK. The harness reads the enum values from the landed cmdq headers rather than
retyping them, and its one transcription - mtk-cmdq-helper.c's private struct
cmdq_instruction and its two write_s helpers - is pinned by a sha256 of those bodies in
the gate log.

Address handling is stock's, not invented: the subsys table is the name column of
v3/cmdq_subsys_common.c:9-68 (44 rows, all compared row by row by the harness) and the
triples are read from the "gce" node of the port's mt6768.dts with
of_property_read_u32_array(node, name, {msb, id, mask}) exactly as
cmdq_dev_get_subsys_by_name() (v3/cmdq_device.c:264) does. No binding is added: no new
property name, no change to #gce-subsys-cells or #gce-event-cells, no new compatible, and
no edit to drivers/mailbox/ or include/linux/mailbox/. The harness confirms the 31 rows
this board's gce node carries are byte-equal to the vendor board's.

Two honest narrowings, both measured:
  - an address no table row covers is rejected (-EINVAL, once to the log) instead of
    taking the vendor's detour through CMDQ_SPR_FOR_TEMP, which needs an SPR allocator and
    a LOGIC-assign instruction this tree's helper does not expose. Truncating 99 into the
    5-bit field would have silently retargeted the write to subsys 3, which is why the
    harness carries that as its own case.
  - register-typed operands are rejected. cmdqRecWrite() takes "u32 value" in this tree
    (v3/cmdq_record.h:167), so the vendor's CMDQ_DATA_BIT tag is 0 by construction and no
    landed callsite can produce a 1; measured, zero references to CMDQ_REG_VALUE,
    CMDQ_REG_EXT_VALUE or cmdq_reg_val_to_reg_str() in video/mt6768 or video/common.
  - the vendor's prefetch policy (cmdq_get_func()->shouldEnablePrefetch(), around
    cmdq_append_command()) is not carried, so a record built here omits the
    prefetch-disable and mark instructions a stock record with prefetch enabled would
    contain. Stated because it is a real difference in the instruction stream, and it is
    the reason the record layer's maturity claim stops at "compiles and links".

Nothing in this tree can build a record: cmdqRecCreate() appears only in the unused
DISP_REG_VAL_SET() macro at ddp_reg.h:272, so the layer is link-required and unreachable at
runtime. That is deliberate - it closes the three symbols the display path needs without
inventing a caller, and it is the state the plan of record describes in
report/l2-record-layer-design-bprime.md section 8.

The event surface is what the device tree says it is: CMDQ_SYNC_TOKEN_CONFIG_DIRTY and
CMDQ_SYNC_TOKEN_STREAM_EOF are software tokens and self-number (640, 641), so both entry
points work with no device tree at all; CMDQ_EVENT_MUTEX0_STREAM_EOF is a hardware event
whose id comes from stream_done_0, which this board's gce node does carry (<130>, identical
to the vendor's). The adapter reads that once from a postcore_initcall rather than lazily
from the callsite, because stock's read happens at its own driver probe and a lazy read
would put of_mutex in front of code the vendor reaches from atomic context.

Gated on the existing CONFIG_MTK_DISP_BRINGUP, next to the slot provider from 0088, so
there is still one switch for the display objects and their providers, and no new Kconfig
symbol. Verified in the 0091 slice gate: switch off, whole-tree link clean and byte-stable
against 0090; switch on, distinct open names 65 -> 62 with the three record names closed
and defined exactly once tree-wide, 0 error: lines, 0 collisions, appended DTB payload and
mt6768.dtb untouched. No functional claim: no device is flashed, no frame is drawn, and
the display path still cannot be exercised until the panel handover and the rest of the
open symbols land.
---
 drivers/soc/mediatek/Makefile                 |  10 +
 drivers/soc/mediatek/mtk-cmdq-disp-record.c   | 440 ++++++++++++++++++
 .../linux/soc/mediatek/mtk-cmdq-disp-record.h | 229 +++++++++
 3 files changed, 679 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 drivers/soc/mediatek/mtk-cmdq-disp-record.c
 create mode 100644 include/linux/soc/mediatek/mtk-cmdq-disp-record.h

diff --git a/drivers/soc/mediatek/Makefile b/drivers/soc/mediatek/Makefile
index e5d69637b..78ae521d3 100644
--- a/drivers/soc/mediatek/Makefile
+++ b/drivers/soc/mediatek/Makefile
@@ -5,6 +5,16 @@ obj-$(CONFIG_MTK_CMDQ) += mtk-cmdq-helper.o
 # there is one switch and no way to enable the objects without their provider - the same arrangement as
 # the CONFIG_MTK_CMDQ_MBOX line below, whose symbol comes from drivers/mailbox/Kconfig.
 obj-$(CONFIG_MTK_DISP_BRINGUP) += mtk-cmdq-disp-slot.o
+# The provider for the three record-mode symbols the ported display core references: cmdqRecWrite
+# (from DISP_REG_SET/DDP_REG in video/mt6768/dispsys/ddp_reg.h) and cmdqRecWaitNoClear /
+# cmdqRecSetEventToken (video/mt6768/dispsys/ddp_path.c:908, :910, :927). Same single switch as the
+# slot provider above, so the display objects and their CMDQ providers move together (slice 0091).
+# It reads the vendor cmdq headers, so it needs the include set the display objects get from
+# drivers/misc/mediatek/video/mt6768/dispsys/Makefile; -I is per object so nothing else in this
+# directory changes what it sees.
+obj-$(CONFIG_MTK_DISP_BRINGUP) += mtk-cmdq-disp-record.o
+CFLAGS_mtk-cmdq-disp-record.o += -I$(srctree)/drivers/misc/mediatek/cmdq/v3 \
+                                 -I$(srctree)/drivers/misc/mediatek/cmdq/v3/mt6768
 obj-$(CONFIG_MTK_DEVAPC) += mtk-devapc.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_MTK_PMQOS) += mtk-pm-qos.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_MTK_CMDQ) += mtk-cmdq-helper.o
diff --git a/drivers/soc/mediatek/mtk-cmdq-disp-record.c b/drivers/soc/mediatek/mtk-cmdq-disp-record.c
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..776a67a26
--- /dev/null
+++ b/drivers/soc/mediatek/mtk-cmdq-disp-record.c
@@ -0,0 +1,440 @@
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
+/*
+ * Record-mode command adapter for the ported MT6768 display path.
+ *
+ * What the landed display code calls, and nothing more (measured over the
+ * built objects with nm -u, not by grep attribution):
+ *
+ *   cmdqRecWrite()        - DISP_REG_SET() / DISP_REG_UPDATA() in
+ *                           video/mt6768/dispsys/ddp_reg.h:266-280, expanded
+ *                           by ddp_module.c, ddp_dpi.c, ddp_ddp.c and others
+ *                           (29 references before this slice, 2 more after it)
+ *   cmdqRecWaitNoClear()  - video/mt6768/dispsys/ddp_path.c:908 and :910, in
+ *                           ddp_insert_config_allow_rec()
+ *   cmdqRecSetEventToken()- video/mt6768/dispsys/ddp_path.c:927, in
+ *                           ddp_insert_config_dirty_rec()
+ *
+ * Each of the three is a thin, faithful copy of what the 4.19.325 v3 engine
+ * does for that name, and each delegates to the mainline mailbox helper this
+ * tree already carries, exactly as the vendor delegates:
+ *
+ *   cmdqRecWrite(handle, addr, value, mask)
+ *     -> cmdq_op_write_reg()   v3/cmdq_record.c:1368   (mask select, opcode
+ *        select, cmdq_var_data_type() at :196)
+ *     -> cmdq_append_command() v3/cmdq_record.c:970
+ *     -> cmdq_append_rw_s_command() :847                (subsys lookup, arg_a
+ *        and arg_b packing)
+ *     -> cmdq_append_command_pkt()  :706                (the 64-bit word)
+ *   cmdqRecWaitNoClear(handle, event)
+ *     -> cmdq_op_wait_no_clear() v3/cmdq_record.c:1510  -> cmdq_pkt_wait_no_clear()
+ *   cmdqRecSetEventToken(handle, event)
+ *     -> cmdq_op_set_event()     v3/cmdq_record.c:1532  -> cmdq_pkt_set_event()
+ *
+ * The two event entry points are pure delegations in the vendor too - they
+ * resolve the enum to an id and call the same helper function this file calls.
+ * The write entry point is where the vendor and this tree genuinely differ,
+ * and report/l2-record-layer-design-bprime.md section 3 is the argument for
+ * why the difference does not change the instruction stream; the harness
+ * upstream-port/tests/mtk_disp_record_host_check.c measures it case by case
+ * instead of leaving it as prose. Short version: the vendor appends
+ * MOVE(arg_b = ~mask) + WRITE_S_W_MASK, mainline appends MASK(mask = ~mask) +
+ * WRITE_S_MASK, and both headers give those opcode pairs the same two numbers
+ * (0x02 and 0x91 - include/linux/mailbox/mtk-cmdq-mailbox.h versus
+ * v3/cmdq_def.h), with the same field placement.
+ *
+ * Deliberate omissions, each one measured rather than assumed:
+ *
+ *  - The vendor's prefetch handling. cmdq_append_command() consults
+ *    cmdq_get_func()->shouldEnablePrefetch() and, when it says yes, inserts a
+ *    prefetch-disable and a mark instruction around each write. That policy
+ *    lives in v3/cmdq_virtual.c, which this port does not build. So this
+ *    adapter emits exactly the write and nothing around it, which is what the
+ *    vendor emits when its prefetch policy is off. A record built here is
+ *    therefore not byte-identical to a record built by the vendor with
+ *    prefetch on; nothing in this tree can observe that today, because no
+ *    landed code calls cmdqRecCreate() (it appears only in the unused
+ *    DISP_REG_VAL_SET() macro at ddp_reg.h:272), so no packet can be created
+ *    and no record can reach hardware. That is the state this slice is shipped
+ *    in: link-required, not reachable at run time.
+ *  - The special-subsys detour. cmdq_append_rw_s_command() takes an address no
+ *    table row covers and loads it into CMDQ_SPR_FOR_TEMP with a
+ *    CMDQ_CODE_LOGIC/ASSIGN instruction, then writes through that register.
+ *    This port has no SPR allocation and the mainline helper has no primitive
+ *    for it, so mtk_disp_rec_resolve_subsys() returns -EINVAL and says so
+ *    instead of truncating the id into the 5-bit field. The two addresses that
+ *    need this on the vendor side (pwm 0x1100e000, 0x1100d000) are the same
+ *    two the survey in report/l2-record-layer-design-bprime.md section 4
+ *    measured as unresolvable, and no landed display object can produce them
+ *    through this adapter: measured, zero references to CMDQ_REG_VALUE,
+ *    CMDQ_REG_EXT_VALUE or cmdq_reg_val_to_reg_str() in video/mt6768 and
+ *    video/common.
+ *  - Register-typed operands. cmdqRecWrite() takes "u32 value" in this tree's
+ *    header (v3/cmdq_record.h:167), so the vendor's CMDQ_DATA_BIT tag is 0 by
+ *    construction and the value_type == 1 branch of cmdq_append_rw_s_command()
+ *    is unreachable from this entry point. It is kept as a rejection so that
+ *    anyone who later lands cmdqRecWriteFromDataRegister() finds the hole
+ *    documented rather than encoded wrongly.
+ *  - cmdq_task_check_available() and the chunked command buffer it serves
+ *    (pkt->buf, avail_buf_size, cmdq_pkt_add_cmd_buffer(), v3/cmdq_record.c:683
+ *    and :706). The mainline packet is one buffer with a size; capacity is the
+ *    helper's business and this file only propagates what it returns.
+ *  - The vendor's CMDQ_ERR()/CMDQ_MSG() logging, its per-scenario CPR book-
+ *    keeping, and cmdq_check_before_append()'s error-string side effects.
+ *    The -EFAULT / -EBUSY / -EINVAL results are kept; the log lines are not.
+ *
+ * The device tree surface is unchanged by this file: it reads the "gce" node
+ * that already exists in arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt6768.dts:1436 and the
+ * property names the vendor engine itself uses. It invents no binding, adds no
+ * cell to #gce-subsys-cells or #gce-event-cells, and registers no compatible.
+ */
+#include <linux/err.h>
+#include <linux/export.h>
+#include <linux/kernel.h>
+#include <linux/mailbox/mtk-cmdq-mailbox.h>
+#include <linux/mutex.h>
+#include <linux/of.h>
+#include <linux/soc/mediatek/mtk-cmdq-disp-record.h>
+#include <linux/soc/mediatek/mtk-cmdq.h>
+#include <linux/types.h>
+
+#include "cmdq_record.h"
+
+/*
+ * Name column of the vendor's subsys table, v3/cmdq_subsys_common.c:9-68,
+ * transcribed without the .group column (the vendor uses it only in a
+ * CMDQ_LOG() line). Indexed by enum CMDQ_SUBSYS_ENUM from the landed
+ * cmdq_subsys_common.h, so a row cannot silently shift; rows not listed here
+ * are NULL, exactly as they are in the vendor table.
+ * report/l2-record-layer-design-bprime.md section 4 measured which of these
+ * the mt6768 device tree actually populates.
+ */
+static const char *const rec_subsys_dts[] = {
+	[CMDQ_SUBSYS_G3D_CONFIG_BASE] = "g3d_config_base",
+	[CMDQ_SUBSYS_MMSYS_CONFIG] = "mmsys_config_base",
+	[CMDQ_SUBSYS_DISP_DITHER] = "disp_dither_base",
+	[CMDQ_SUBSYS_NA] = "mm_na_base",
+	[CMDQ_SUBSYS_IMGSYS] = "imgsys_base",
+	[CMDQ_SUBSYS_VDEC_GCON] = "vdec_gcon_base",
+	[CMDQ_SUBSYS_VENC_GCON] = "venc_gcon_base",
+	[CMDQ_SUBSYS_CONN_PERIPHERALS] = "conn_peri_base",
+	[CMDQ_SUBSYS_TOPCKGEN] = "topckgen_base",
+	[CMDQ_SUBSYS_KP] = "kp_base",
+	[CMDQ_SUBSYS_SCP_SRAM] = "scp_sram_base",
+	[CMDQ_SUBSYS_INFRA_NA3] = "infra_na3_base",
+	[CMDQ_SUBSYS_INFRA_NA4] = "infra_na4_base",
+	[CMDQ_SUBSYS_SCP] = "scp_base",
+	[CMDQ_SUBSYS_MCUCFG] = "mcucfg_base",
+	[CMDQ_SUBSYS_GCPU] = "gcpu_base",
+	[CMDQ_SUBSYS_USB0] = "usb0_base",
+	[CMDQ_SUBSYS_USB_SIF] = "usb_sif_base",
+	[CMDQ_SUBSYS_AUDIO] = "audio_base",
+	[CMDQ_SUBSYS_MSDC0] = "msdc0_base",
+	[CMDQ_SUBSYS_MSDC1] = "msdc1_base",
+	[CMDQ_SUBSYS_MSDC2] = "msdc2_base",
+	[CMDQ_SUBSYS_MSDC3] = "msdc3_base",
+	[CMDQ_SUBSYS_AP_DMA] = "ap_dma_base",
+	[CMDQ_SUBSYS_GCE] = "gce_base",
+	[CMDQ_SUBSYS_VDEC] = "vdec_base",
+	[CMDQ_SUBSYS_VDEC1] = "vdec1_base",
+	[CMDQ_SUBSYS_VDEC2] = "vdec2_base",
+	[CMDQ_SUBSYS_VDEC3] = "vdec3_base",
+	[CMDQ_SUBSYS_CAMSYS] = "camsys_base",
+	[CMDQ_SUBSYS_CAMSYS1] = "camsys1_base",
+	[CMDQ_SUBSYS_CAMSYS2] = "camsys2_base",
+	[CMDQ_SUBSYS_CAMSYS3] = "camsys3_base",
+	[CMDQ_SUBSYS_IMGSYS1] = "imgsys1_base",
+	[CMDQ_SUBSYS_SMI_LAB1] = "smi_larb1_base",
+	[CMDQ_SUBSYS_PWM_SW] = "pwm_sw_base",
+	[CMDQ_SUBSYS_PWM1_SW] = "pwm1_sw_base",
+	[CMDQ_SUBSYS_DIP_A0_SW] = "dip_a0_sw_base",
+	[CMDQ_SUBSYS_MIPITX0] = "mipitx0_base",
+	[CMDQ_SUBSYS_MIPITX1] = "mipitx1_base",
+	[CMDQ_SUBSYS_VENC] = "venc_base",
+	[CMDQ_SUBSYS_IMGSYS2] = "imgsys2_base",
+	[CMDQ_SUBSYS_IPESYS] = "ipesys_base",
+	[CMDQ_SUBSYS_DPE] = "dpe_base",
+};
+
+/*
+ * The vendor table is sized by CMDQ_SUBSYS_MAX_COUNT (v3/cmdq_def.h:307) and
+ * its last named row is CMDQ_SUBSYS_DPE, so sizing ours the same way makes the
+ * lookup loop walk exactly the rows cmdq_core_subsys_from_phys_addr() walks.
+ */
+#define REC_SUBSYS_COUNT	CMDQ_SUBSYS_MAX_COUNT
+
+/*
+ * Hardware events that need the device tree, and the property each one is read
+ * from. Every row is a DECLAR_EVENT() line from v3/cmdq_event_common.c, and
+ * only the events a landed display object passes are listed: measured, the
+ * tree references exactly one - CMDQ_EVENT_MUTEX0_STREAM_EOF at
+ * ddp_path.c:908, declared at v3/cmdq_event_common.c:147 as "stream_done_0".
+ * Growing this list is a copy of the vendor's line, nothing else.
+ */
+struct rec_hw_event {
+	enum cmdq_event event;
+	const char *dts_name;
+};
+
+static const struct rec_hw_event rec_hw_events[] = {
+	{ CMDQ_EVENT_MUTEX0_STREAM_EOF, "stream_done_0" },
+};
+
+static struct mtk_disp_rec_subsys rec_subsys[REC_SUBSYS_COUNT];
+
+static s32 rec_hw_event_id[ARRAY_SIZE(rec_hw_events)];
+static bool rec_dts_ready;
+static DEFINE_MUTEX(rec_dts_lock);
+
+/*
+ * cmdq_dev_get_subsys_by_name() (v3/cmdq_device.c:264) and
+ * cmdq_dev_get_event_value_by_name() (v3/cmdq_device.c:336) read the same
+ * properties the same way. The vendor does it once, from its own driver's
+ * probe, because it owns the device that the "gce" node describes; this port
+ * has no such driver to hang it on, so the read happens once from a late
+ * initcall and every record call afterwards is lock-free and non-sleeping.
+ * Reading it lazily from the callsite instead would put an of_mutex path in
+ * front of code that the vendor reaches from atomic context.
+ */
+static void rec_read_dts(struct device_node *node)
+{
+	u32 cell[3];
+	u32 i;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < REC_SUBSYS_COUNT; i++) {
+		rec_subsys[i].subsys_id = -1;
+		if (!rec_subsys_dts[i])
+			continue;
+		if (of_property_read_u32_array(node, rec_subsys_dts[i], cell,
+					       ARRAY_SIZE(cell)) < 0)
+			continue;
+		rec_subsys[i].msb = cell[0] & cell[2];
+		rec_subsys[i].subsys_id = cell[1];
+		rec_subsys[i].mask = cell[2];
+	}
+
+	for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(rec_hw_events); i++) {
+		u32 val = 0;
+
+		/*
+		 * cmdq_dev_get_event_value_by_name() leaves the default in
+		 * place when the property is missing; -1 marks exactly that
+		 * "the device tree did not speak" state here, and is the value
+		 * mtk_disp_rec_event_default() already made unusable.
+		 */
+		if (of_property_read_u32(node, rec_hw_events[i].dts_name,
+					 &val) < 0)
+			rec_hw_event_id[i] = -1;
+		else
+			rec_hw_event_id[i] = (s32)val;
+	}
+}
+
+static int __init mtk_disp_rec_init(void)
+{
+	struct device_node *node;
+
+	/* Guards the indexing below against a new row in the vendor enum. */
+	BUILD_BUG_ON(REC_SUBSYS_COUNT != ARRAY_SIZE(rec_subsys_dts));
+
+	node = of_find_node_by_name(NULL, "gce");
+	if (!node) {
+		pr_info("mtk-cmdq-disp-record: no \"gce\" node, record writes will be refused\n");
+		return 0;
+	}
+
+	mutex_lock(&rec_dts_lock);
+	rec_read_dts(node);
+	rec_dts_ready = true;
+	mutex_unlock(&rec_dts_lock);
+
+	of_node_put(node);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+/*
+ * postcore_initcall() so the table is in place before any display driver
+ * initcall can build a record; the vendor's equivalent read happens at its own
+ * cmdq device probe, which this tree has no instance of.
+ */
+postcore_initcall(mtk_disp_rec_init);
+
+static s32 mtk_disp_rec_resolve_subsys(u32 addr, u32 *subsys)
+{
+	s32 id;
+
+	if (!READ_ONCE(rec_dts_ready))
+		return -EAGAIN;
+
+	id = mtk_disp_rec_core_subsys_from_phys_addr(rec_subsys,
+						     REC_SUBSYS_COUNT, addr);
+	if (id == MTK_DISP_REC_SUBSYS_SPECIAL) {
+		pr_err_once("mtk-cmdq-disp-record: address 0x%08x is not in the gce subsys table\n",
+			    addr);
+		return -EINVAL;
+	}
+	if (id < 0 || id > MTK_DISP_REC_SUBSYS_WIDTH_MASK) {
+		pr_err_once("mtk-cmdq-disp-record: subsys id %d does not fit the instruction field\n",
+			    id);
+		return -EINVAL;
+	}
+
+	*subsys = id;
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+/*
+ * cmdq_get_event_op_id(), v3/cmdq_record.c:1481, with cmdq_core_get_event_value()
+ * (v3/cmdq_helper_ext.c:2314) folded into the table it would have read.
+ */
+static s32 mtk_disp_rec_event_op_id(enum cmdq_event event)
+{
+	s32 id = event;
+	u32 i;
+
+	if (event < 0 || event >= CMDQ_SYNC_TOKEN_MAX) {
+		pr_err_once("mtk-cmdq-disp-record: invalid event %d\n", event);
+		return -EINVAL;
+	}
+
+	id = mtk_disp_rec_event_default(event);
+	if (id < 0) {
+		for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(rec_hw_events); i++) {
+			if (rec_hw_events[i].event != event)
+				continue;
+			id = READ_ONCE(rec_hw_event_id[i]);
+			break;
+		}
+	}
+
+	if (id < 0) {
+		pr_err_once("mtk-cmdq-disp-record: event %d has no id in this device tree\n",
+			    event);
+		return -EINVAL;
+	}
+
+	return id;
+}
+
+/*
+ * Same guards as cmdq_check_before_append() (v3/cmdq_record.c:694): no handle
+ * or no packet is -EFAULT, a flushed record is -EBUSY. The vendor's
+ * cmdq_task_check_available() buffer refill step is not reproduced, because
+ * the mainline packet is a single fixed buffer - see the comment above
+ * mtk_disp_rec_write_s().
+ */
+static struct cmdq_pkt *mtk_disp_rec_pkt(struct cmdqRecStruct *handle)
+{
+	if (!handle || !handle->pkt)
+		return ERR_PTR(-EFAULT);
+	if (handle->finalized)
+		return ERR_PTR(-EBUSY);
+
+	return handle->pkt;
+}
+
+static int mtk_disp_rec_write_s(struct cmdq_pkt *pkt, u32 addr, u32 value,
+				u32 subsys, u32 mask)
+{
+	if (mask != 0xffffffff)
+		return cmdq_pkt_write_s_mask_value(pkt, subsys,
+						   addr & MTK_DISP_REC_ADDR_MASK,
+						   value, mask);
+
+	return cmdq_pkt_write_s_value(pkt, (u8)subsys,
+				      addr & MTK_DISP_REC_ADDR_MASK, value);
+}
+
+/**
+ * cmdqRecWrite() - append one GCE register write to a recorded request
+ * @handle: record created by cmdqRecCreate() (no landed code can create one)
+ * @addr:   physical address of the register, subsys base included
+ * @value:  value to write
+ * @mask:   write mask; %UINT_MAX writes all bits
+ *
+ * Transcription of cmdqRecWrite() -> cmdq_op_write_reg() (v3/cmdq_record.c:1368
+ * and the two lines that follow it in that function).
+ *
+ * Return: 0 on success, a negative error code if the record is unusable, the
+ * address is not in the device tree's subsys table, the value carries a
+ * register tag this entry point cannot receive, or the packet is full.
+ */
+s32 cmdqRecWrite(struct cmdqRecStruct *handle, u32 addr, u32 value, u32 mask)
+{
+	struct cmdq_pkt *pkt;
+	u32 arg_b, arg_b_type, subsys;
+	s32 ret;
+
+	pkt = mtk_disp_rec_pkt(handle);
+	if (IS_ERR(pkt))
+		return PTR_ERR(pkt);
+
+	ret = mtk_disp_rec_var_data_type((u64)value, &arg_b, &arg_b_type);
+	if (ret < 0)
+		return ret;
+	if (arg_b_type) {
+		pr_err_once("mtk-cmdq-disp-record: register operand not supported\n");
+		return -EOPNOTSUPP;
+	}
+
+	ret = mtk_disp_rec_resolve_subsys(addr, &subsys);
+	if (ret < 0)
+		return ret;
+
+	return mtk_disp_rec_write_s(pkt, addr, value, subsys, mask);
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL(cmdqRecWrite);
+
+/**
+ * cmdqRecWaitNoClear() - wait for an event without consuming it
+ * @handle: record created by cmdqRecCreate()
+ * @event:  the event to wait for
+ *
+ * Transcription of cmdqRecWaitNoClear() -> cmdq_op_wait_no_clear()
+ * (v3/cmdq_record.c:1510), including its -EINVAL when the event has no id.
+ *
+ * Return: 0 on success or a negative error code.
+ */
+s32 cmdqRecWaitNoClear(struct cmdqRecStruct *handle, enum cmdq_event event)
+{
+	struct cmdq_pkt *pkt;
+	s32 arg_a;
+
+	arg_a = mtk_disp_rec_event_op_id(event);
+	if (arg_a < 0 || !handle)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	pkt = mtk_disp_rec_pkt(handle);
+	if (IS_ERR(pkt))
+		return PTR_ERR(pkt);
+
+	return cmdq_pkt_wait_no_clear(pkt, (u16)arg_a);
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL(cmdqRecWaitNoClear);
+
+/**
+ * cmdqRecSetEventToken() - raise an event, unconditionally
+ * @handle: record created by cmdqRecCreate()
+ * @event:  the event to set
+ *
+ * Transcription of cmdqRecSetEventToken() -> cmdq_op_set_event()
+ * (v3/cmdq_record.c:1532).
+ *
+ * Return: 0 on success or a negative error code.
+ */
+s32 cmdqRecSetEventToken(struct cmdqRecStruct *handle, enum cmdq_event event)
+{
+	struct cmdq_pkt *pkt;
+	s32 arg_a;
+
+	arg_a = mtk_disp_rec_event_op_id(event);
+	if (arg_a < 0 || !handle)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	pkt = mtk_disp_rec_pkt(handle);
+	if (IS_ERR(pkt))
+		return PTR_ERR(pkt);
+
+	return cmdq_pkt_set_event(pkt, (u16)arg_a);
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL(cmdqRecSetEventToken);
diff --git a/include/linux/soc/mediatek/mtk-cmdq-disp-record.h b/include/linux/soc/mediatek/mtk-cmdq-disp-record.h
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..cd6170bd9
--- /dev/null
+++ b/include/linux/soc/mediatek/mtk-cmdq-disp-record.h
@@ -0,0 +1,229 @@
+/* SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only */
+/*
+ * Instruction encoding rules for the MT6768 display record adapter.
+ *
+ * This header exists so that the encoding can be exercised on the host
+ * (upstream-port/tests/mtk_disp_record_host_check.c) against the vendor's
+ * own arithmetic, and not only by reading a .c file. Everything below is a
+ * transcription of a named line of the 4.19.325 v3 CMDQ engine, in the
+ * vendor's terms, with the vendor's constants; nothing here adds a rule.
+ *
+ *   subsys position inside arg_a ......... cmdq/v3/cmdq_virtual.c:170
+ *                                        (getSubsysLSBArgA() returns 16 on
+ *                                         mt6768; other platforms use 18)
+ *   arg_a = (addr & 0xffff) | subsys<<16 . cmdq/v3/cmdq_record.c:847
+ *                                        (cmdq_append_rw_s_command, with
+ *                                         arg_type placed at bit 21)
+ *   arg_b = value, or reg << 16 ......... cmdq/v3/cmdq_record.c:847 (same
+ *                                        function, value_type selects which)
+ *   64-bit word = ((code<<24 | arg_a)<<32) | arg_b
+ *                                ......... cmdq/v3/cmdq_record.c:706
+ *                                        (cmdq_append_command_pkt)
+ *   value classification ................. cmdq/v3/cmdq_record.c:196
+ *                                        (cmdq_var_data_type; CMDQ_DATA_BIT
+ *                                         and the two bit values come from
+ *                                         cmdq_record.h:25-27)
+ *   "address not in the table" marker .... cmdq/v3/cmdq_def.h:24
+ *                                        (CMDQ_SPECIAL_SUBSYS_ADDR = 99)
+ *   event id defaults .................... cmdq/v3/cmdq_helper_ext.c:2265
+ *                                        (cmdq_core_init_dts_data: a table
+ *                                         index <= CMDQ_MAX_HW_EVENT_COUNT is
+ *                                         unusable until the device tree fills
+ *                                         it in, an index above it is its own
+ *                                         id -- that is why the SW sync tokens
+ *                                         need no device tree at all)
+ *
+ * No kernel lock here, no memory allocation, no device tree access: if a
+ * future slice wants these rules for another engine it can call them the same
+ * way the host harness does.
+ */
+#ifndef __LINUX_SOC_MEDIATEK_MTK_CMDQ_DISP_RECORD_H
+#define __LINUX_SOC_MEDIATEK_MTK_CMDQ_DISP_RECORD_H
+
+#include <linux/errno.h>
+#include <linux/types.h>
+
+/*
+ * cmdq/v3/cmdq_def.h:24. cmdq_core_subsys_from_phys_addr() returns this when
+ * no table row covers the address; cmdq_append_rw_s_command() then diverts the
+ * write through the special-purpose register (CMDQ_CODE_LOGIC + ASSIGN into
+ * CMDQ_SPR_FOR_TEMP). This port cannot express that diversion - see the
+ * comment above mtk_disp_rec_resolve_subsys().
+ */
+#define MTK_DISP_REC_SUBSYS_SPECIAL		99
+
+/* cmdq/v3/cmdq_virtual.c:170 for mt6768. */
+#define MTK_DISP_REC_SUBSYS_BIT			16
+
+/*
+ * The subsys field is 5 bits wide; both cmdq_append_rw_s_command()
+ * ("(subsys & 0x1f) << subsys_bit") and struct cmdq_instruction's "u8 sop:5"
+ * agree on that, and 99 (0x63) does not fit, which is why an unresolved
+ * address must be rejected before packing rather than truncated.
+ */
+#define MTK_DISP_REC_SUBSYS_WIDTH_MASK		0x1f
+
+/* Low 16 bits of arg_a hold the register offset (cmdq_record.c:847). */
+#define MTK_DISP_REC_ADDR_MASK			0xffff
+
+/* cmdq_record.c:847 packs arg_type (addr type bit 2, value type bit 1) here. */
+#define MTK_DISP_REC_ARG_TYPE_SHIFT		21
+
+/* cmdq_record.h:25-27: CMDQ_DATA_BIT, CMDQ_BIT_VALUE, CMDQ_BIT_VAR. */
+#define MTK_DISP_REC_DATA_BIT			62
+#define MTK_DISP_REC_BIT_VALUE			0
+#define MTK_DISP_REC_BIT_VAR			1
+
+/* One GCE instruction, in bytes (CMDQ_INST_SIZE in the mailbox header). */
+#define MTK_DISP_REC_INST_SIZE			8
+
+/* cmdq/v3/cmdq_event_common.h:504 and :656. */
+#define MTK_DISP_REC_MAX_HW_EVENT		512
+#define MTK_DISP_REC_TOKEN_MAX			0x3ff
+
+/**
+ * struct mtk_disp_rec_subsys - one row of the GCE subsys base table.
+ * @msb:	 bits every address in this subsys must share (@base & @mask)
+ * @subsys_id:   the 5-bit value written into arg_a, -1 if the row is unused
+ * @mask:	 compare mask taken from the third device tree cell
+ *
+ * Same three fields, same derivation, as struct SubsysStruct
+ * (cmdq/v3/cmdq_def.h:296) filled by cmdq_dev_get_subsys_by_name()
+ * (cmdq/v3/cmdq_device.c:264). Kept separate because the vendor's struct
+ * carries a 30-byte group name used only by a CMDQ_LOG() line.
+ */
+struct mtk_disp_rec_subsys {
+	u32 msb;
+	s32 subsys_id;
+	u32 mask;
+};
+
+/**
+ * mtk_disp_rec_core_subsys_from_phys_addr() - which subsys owns a bus address
+ * @table: rows indexed by enum CMDQ_SUBSYS_ENUM, as in the vendor engine
+ * @count: number of rows in @table
+ * @pa:    the physical address the caller wants to write
+ *
+ * Verbatim semantics of cmdq_core_subsys_from_phys_addr()
+ * (cmdq/v3/cmdq_helper_ext.c:2515): walk the rows, skip the ones the device
+ * tree did not fill in, and compare (@pa & @mask) with the row's @msb.
+ *
+ * Return: the subsys id, or %MTK_DISP_REC_SUBSYS_SPECIAL if no row covers it.
+ */
+static inline s32
+mtk_disp_rec_core_subsys_from_phys_addr(const struct mtk_disp_rec_subsys *table,
+					u32 count, u32 pa)
+{
+	u32 i;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < count; i++) {
+		if (table[i].subsys_id == -1)
+			continue;
+		if ((pa & table[i].mask) == table[i].msb)
+			return table[i].subsys_id;
+	}
+
+	return MTK_DISP_REC_SUBSYS_SPECIAL;
+}
+
+/**
+ * mtk_disp_rec_pack_arg_a() - build arg_a for a READ_S/WRITE_S instruction
+ * @addr_low: register offset inside the subsys (low 16 bits are used)
+ * @subsys:   subsys id selected by the table above
+ * @arg_type: (addr_type << 2) | (value_type << 1), as the vendor computes it
+ *
+ * Return: arg_a, still needing the opcode at bits 24-31 (see
+ * mtk_disp_rec_inst_word()).
+ */
+static inline u32 mtk_disp_rec_pack_arg_a(u32 addr_low, u32 subsys,
+					  u32 arg_type)
+{
+	return (addr_low & MTK_DISP_REC_ADDR_MASK) |
+	       ((subsys & MTK_DISP_REC_SUBSYS_WIDTH_MASK) <<
+		MTK_DISP_REC_SUBSYS_BIT) |
+	       (arg_type << MTK_DISP_REC_ARG_TYPE_SHIFT);
+}
+
+/**
+ * mtk_disp_rec_pack_arg_b() - build arg_b for a READ_S/WRITE_S instruction
+ * @value:	 the 32-bit value, or the register index if @value_type is 1
+ * @value_type: 0 for an immediate, 1 for a register operand
+ *
+ * Verbatim from cmdq_append_rw_s_command(): a register operand is shifted into
+ * the upper half of arg_b, an immediate is placed as is.
+ */
+static inline u32 mtk_disp_rec_pack_arg_b(u32 value, u32 value_type)
+{
+	return value_type ? value << 16 : value;
+}
+
+/**
+ * mtk_disp_rec_inst_word() - assemble the 64-bit instruction
+ * @code:  opcode, placed at bits 24-31 of the high word
+ * @arg_a: packed as above
+ * @arg_b: packed as above
+ *
+ * cmdq/v3/cmdq_record.c:706, one line, kept one line:
+ *	*va = (u64)((code << CMDQ_OP_CODE_SHIFT) | arg_a) << 32 | arg_b;
+ */
+static inline u64 mtk_disp_rec_inst_word(u32 code, u32 arg_a, u32 arg_b)
+{
+	return ((u64)((code << 24) | arg_a) << 32) | arg_b;
+}
+
+/**
+ * mtk_disp_rec_var_data_type() - is the operand an immediate or a register?
+ * @arg_in:	 the vendor's CMDQ_VARIABLE, unmodified
+ * @arg_out:	 the low 32 bits, what actually goes into the instruction
+ * @arg_type:	 0 for immediate, 1 for register
+ *
+ * Transcription of cmdq_var_data_type() (cmdq/v3/cmdq_record.c:196),
+ * including its rejection of any other tag.
+ *
+ * Return: 0 on success, -EFAULT if the tag is neither value nor var.
+ */
+static inline s32 mtk_disp_rec_var_data_type(u64 arg_in, u32 *arg_out,
+					     u32 *arg_type)
+{
+	switch (arg_in >> MTK_DISP_REC_DATA_BIT) {
+	case MTK_DISP_REC_BIT_VALUE:
+		*arg_type = 0;
+		*arg_out = (u32)(arg_in & 0xffffffff);
+		return 0;
+	case MTK_DISP_REC_BIT_VAR:
+		*arg_type = 1;
+		*arg_out = (u32)(arg_in & 0xffffffff);
+		return 0;
+	default:
+		*arg_type = 0;
+		*arg_out = 0;
+		return -EFAULT;
+	}
+}
+
+/**
+ * mtk_disp_rec_event_default() - the id an event has before the device tree
+ *				 speaks
+ * @event: an index into the vendor's event table
+ *
+ * cmdq/v3/cmdq_helper_ext.c:2265-2273 fills the whole table with
+ * CMDQ_SYNC_TOKEN_INVALID - 1 - i for a hardware event and with i itself for a
+ * software sync token. CMDQ_SYNC_TOKEN_INVALID is -1
+ * (cmdq/v3/cmdq_event_common.h:657, landed), so the hardware default is
+ * -2 - i: negative, which is what makes cmdq_get_event_op_id() refuse the
+ * event until the device tree names it. The comparison in the vendor loop is
+ * "<=" (index 512 counts as hardware), reproduced here rather than tidied up.
+ * Expressing the rule as a function instead of a 0x3ff-entry array is the one
+ * liberty this port takes, and the host harness checks it index by index for
+ * all 0x3ff indices against the vendor formula.
+ *
+ * Return: the id, negative if the device tree has to supply one.
+ */
+static inline s32 mtk_disp_rec_event_default(s32 event)
+{
+	if ((u32)event <= MTK_DISP_REC_MAX_HW_EVENT)
+		return -2 - event;
+	return event;
+}
+
+#endif /* __LINUX_SOC_MEDIATEK_MTK_CMDQ_DISP_RECORD_H */
